of the circle one of the angles  is again 90 degrees so we can go back to  using those trigonometry formulas and  then lastly I knew the length of that  little part there because I knew the  ratios that I wanted to split up the  circle in so again using that  information I can I can calculate the  angle and going back to polar  coordinates use it to locate the the two  points and break up the circle into two  arcs so going beyond by triangles in a  more generic use case where you're not  you're working with a triangle that's  not a right triangle in order to solve  that triangle you need slightly more  information so with a right triangle you  just need two pieces of information for  all other kinds of triangles you need at  least three pieces of information so  whether that's three sides or two sides  at an angle or two angles an aside any  of those work and in that scenario you  can use a formula called law of cosines  there similarly something called law of  cost law of sine which looks a lot  similar you can use either or so in this  case you can pick and choose which sites  that you have and which angles you have  and apply to this formula and figure out  all of the other information of the  triangle so let's look at an example  where I use this so a few months ago I  built this animation which essentially  it's using a generative graphic concept  known as metabolic metabolic fat which  meant having to do a lot of math to  figure out how to draw this and what it  comes down to is essentially there are  two circles and then a little white  connector that sort of allows you to  mimic this blobby  shape to it I won't go into all the  details of how this happened I have a  link to a blog post down there that goes  into that but I want to focus on one  specific part so one of the steps in  order to do this was to figure out these  two angles u1 and u2 and these angles  are formed by three point by a triangle  consisting of three points which are the  two centers of the circle and the point  where the two circles intersect so again  I kind of know two sides because there  are the radius of the two circles and  the third side is simply the distance  between the two circles and since I'm  animating these and I'm moving these  things around I know the location of  those two circles that's where the  center's are so I can calculate that  distance and then apply to that law of  cosines formula to figure out the value  for u1 and u2 and be able to figure out  how to draw that connector so I just  want to end by sharing a little resource  so when I was in first learning  programming I learned it through a  language called processing and there's a  book that was that came out when I was  in school called generative design  because I'm in Austria I'm not even  going to attempt to pronounce the German  name here and be sure it but it was a  really amazing resource that goes into a  lot of these mathematical concepts but  and I had a lot of code snippets that  showed you how to do these things with  processing well they're coming out with  a new version later this year which is  built for p5.js so is more to JavaScript  and more  the web the book comes out in a few  months but for now they actually have a  resource up which has a lot of the the  animation examples and which are  interactive and all the code samples  available for you which you can use and  actually see start playing around with  it and learning a lot of these  techniques that are covered here and  that's everything I had thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

